CAT = "caguardIndustry"; SEG = "IND"
# Syllabus sections 1 and 2: the security industry, PSISA licensing and the Code of Conduct.
# Each item: (stem, KEY, [three wrong options], explanation). build.py rotates the key's letter.
Q = [
("Which Ontario law licenses and regulates security guards?",
 "The PSISA, 2005", ["The Police Services Act", "The Trespass to Property Act", "The Security Guards Act, 1990"],
 "The Private Security and Investigative Services Act, 2005 (PSISA) sets licensing, conduct and uniform rules for guards and private investigators. The Trespass to Property Act gives authority on premises but does not license anyone."),
("What is the minimum age for an Ontario security guard licence?",
 "18", ["16", "19", "21"],
 "You must be at least 18 to hold an individual security guard licence. 19 is Ontario's drinking age, not a licensing age."),
("In what form is an Ontario security guard licence now issued?",
 "A digital licence by email", ["A plastic photo card", "A metal badge by mail", "A paper card at ServiceOntario"],
 "Physical cards are no longer issued; the licence is digital and you keep a digital or printed copy. A metal badge is a symbol of authority guards may not carry."),
("While on duty, what must you do with your licence?",
 "Carry it and show it on request", ["Keep it on file at head office", "Show it only to police officers", "Display it on a lanyard only"],
 "PSISA requires a guard to carry the licence at all times on duty and produce it when asked. That includes plain-clothes guards, not only those in uniform."),
("How long does an individual guard licence last before renewal?",
 "Two birthdays after issue", ["One year from the issue date", "Five years from the issue date", "For life if no offences occur"],
 "The expiry is tied to your date of birth and falls two birthdays after the licence is issued, so a first licence can run a little under two years. Renewals then run two years."),
("Which paid worker needs an Ontario security guard licence?",
 "A nightclub bouncer", ["A volunteer event usher", "A cashier watching a till", "A building maintenance worker"],
 "Anyone paid to work primarily protecting people or property needs a licence, including bouncers, loss prevention staff and patrol guards. A cashier's main job is sales, not protection."),
("Which title may a licensed guard use to describe themselves?",
 "Security guard", ["Security officer", "Private detective", "Law enforcement agent"],
 "PSISA bars guards from using the terms officer, police, law enforcement, detective and private detective. Guards identify themselves as security guards."),
("Which item may a guard NOT carry as a sign of authority?",
 "A metal badge", ["A name tag", "A flashlight", "A pocket notebook"],
 "A guard's only symbols of authority are the licence and the uniform, and a metal badge is prohibited because it suggests police authority. A name or licence number tag is actually required on the uniform."),
("What wording must appear on the chest of a guard's uniform?",
 "SECURITY or SECURITY GUARD", ["OFFICER or SECURITY OFFICER", "PATROL or PATROL OFFICER", "AGENT or PROTECTION AGENT"],
 "The uniform regulation requires SECURITY or SECURITY GUARD (or the French equivalents) on the chest of the outermost garment. Officer is one of the terms guards may not use."),
("How tall must the SECURITY lettering on the uniform chest be at minimum?",
 "1.5 cm", ["0.5 cm", "3 cm", "5 cm"],
 "The letters must be at least 1.5 cm high, in upper case and in a colour that contrasts with the uniform. Smaller lettering would not identify the guard at a glance."),
("Which uniform feature is prohibited for a security guard?",
 "A police-style forage cap", ["An ID tag with a licence number", "A reflective safety vest", "The employer's logo or crest"],
 "Features that make a guard look like police, such as forage caps, rank chevrons and trouser stripes, are prohibited. The employer's name or logo and an ID tag are required, not banned."),
("Which guards may normally work without wearing a uniform?",
 "Bodyguards and loss prevention", ["Mobile patrol and alarm guards", "Concierge and front desk guards", "Night shift site guards"],
 "Bodyguards and loss prevention staff may work in plain clothes because their role depends on not standing out. Other guards must wear the uniform, and every guard must still carry the licence."),
("How soon must a licensed guard report a conviction for a listed offence?",
 "Within 5 days", ["Within 24 hours", "Within 30 days", "At the next renewal"],
 "Ontario requires licence holders to report a conviction or finding of guilt for an offence listed in the regulation within 5 days. Waiting for renewal would leave an ineligible person working."),
("What is the maximum penalty for an individual convicted of a PSISA offence?",
 "$25,000, a year in jail, or both", ["$5,000 or 30 days in jail", "$50,000, two years in jail, or both", "A $1,000 fine with no jail"],
 "An individual convicted under PSISA faces a fine of up to $25,000, imprisonment of up to one year, or both. Corporations face higher fines."),
("Under the Code of Conduct, a guard on duty must not:",
 "Be impaired by alcohol", ["Ask visitors to sign in", "Carry a pocket notebook", "Call police about a theft"],
 "The Code of Conduct forbids being under the influence of alcohol or drugs while on duty. Signing in visitors and calling police are routine, proper duties."),
("Which behaviour breaks the Code of Conduct even if no one is hurt?",
 "Swearing at a customer", ["Refusing entry under site policy", "Asking a visitor for their name", "Calling police about a fight"],
 "The Code of Conduct requires guards to avoid profane, abusive or insulting language. Refusing entry under a lawful site policy is part of the job."),
("Where can the public complain about a licensed guard's conduct?",
 "To the Registrar under PSISA", ["To the Ontario Ombudsman", "To the municipal council", "To the Law Society of Ontario"],
 "Complaints go to the Registrar, through the Private Security and Investigative Services Branch, which can resolve, warn, charge or revoke a licence. The Ombudsman oversees public bodies, not private guards."),
("When did the PSISA come into force?",
 "August 23, 2007", ["April 15, 2010", "January 1, 2005", "September 30, 2023"],
 "PSISA took effect on August 23, 2007. April 15, 2010 is when mandatory training and testing began."),
("When did mandatory basic training and testing for guards begin?",
 "April 15, 2010", ["August 23, 2007", "January 1, 2012", "September 30, 2023"],
 "The Training and Testing Regulation took effect on April 15, 2010. PSISA itself had come into force in 2007."),
("Which four core tasks sum up a security guard's role?",
 "Observe, deter, record, report", ["Arrest, search, detain, charge", "Patrol, enforce, fine, report", "Watch, warn, arrest, testify"],
 "Guards mainly observe, deter, record and report; physical intervention is occasional. Laying charges and enforcing laws are police roles."),
("What is the minimum basic training for a guard, including emergency first aid?",
 "40 hours", ["33.5 hours", "20 hours", "60 hours"],
 "Basic training must be at least 40 hours with Emergency Level First Aid included. 33.5 hours is the minimum for someone who already holds a valid first aid certificate."),
("What do you need before you can book the ministry's basic test?",
 "A training completion number", ["A licence from another province", "A letter from a police service", "A job offer from an agency"],
 "Your training provider gives you an Ontario training completion number, which you use to book the test. A job offer is not required to train, test or apply."),
("How many multiple-choice questions are on the basic security guard test?",
 "60", ["40", "50", "100"],
 "The ministry test has 60 multiple-choice questions with 75 minutes to answer them. The questions are based on the basic training syllabus."),
("Which of these could stop you getting a guard licence?",
 "An unpardoned conviction", ["A speeding ticket last year", "Unpaid municipal parking fines", "Holding permanent residency"],
 "You need a clean criminal record: an unpardoned conviction for an offence listed in the regulation makes you ineligible, and others are reviewed. Permanent residents are entitled to work in Canada, so they can apply."),
("Does an in-house guard employed directly by a store need a licence?",
 "Yes, the same licence", ["No, only contract guards do", "Only if they carry handcuffs", "Only if they patrol outdoors"],
 "PSISA requires every individual working as a security guard to be licensed, whether employed by an agency or directly by the business. The employer of in-house guards must also register."),
("A guard employed by an agency and posted to a client's site is called:",
 "A contract guard", ["An in-house guard", "A special constable", "An auxiliary officer"],
 "A contract guard works for a security agency that sells guarding services to clients. An in-house, or proprietary, guard is employed by the business being protected."),
("Which statement about a guard's legal authority is true?",
 "They have no police powers", ["They are peace officers on site", "They may enforce all bylaws", "They gain police powers on duty"],
 "Guards are not police or peace officers; their authority comes from the Criminal Code powers anyone has and from acting as the property owner's agent. Holding out as police is an offence under PSISA."),
("How early can you renew an Ontario guard licence before it expires?",
 "Up to 90 days", ["Up to 30 days", "Up to 7 days", "Up to 180 days"],
 "Ontario lets you renew up to 90 calendar days before the expiry date, and the renewal runs two years from that expiry. Renewing late means you cannot work until it is done."),
]
